<header class="site-header">
  <p class="eyebrow">Explanation</p>
  <h1>Parametric sources</h1>
  <p>
    Parametric sources are ufo font files organised around specific typographic
    transformations, rather than complete named styles.
  </p>
</header>

<div class="intro-content">
  <section>
    <h2>Sources for specific transformations</h2>

    <p>
      In a conventional interpolation workflow, a master usually represents a
      complete location in a design space: a particular combination of weight,
      width, optical size, and other design decisions. (MyFont Condensed Bold Italic)
    </p>

    <p>
      A parametric source also functions as a master, <em>extreme of the axis,</em> but its purpose is more
      specific. It records how one defined transformation should be realised in
      the glyphs. That transformation may concern a stroke, a proportion, an
      alignment, a serif, or another part of the design.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>From axis to source</h2>

    <p>
      A design axis defines what is intended to change. A parametric source
      provides the design information needed to make that change: measurements,
      glyph constructions, outline adjustments, and any related values required
      to preserve the intended behaviour.
    </p>

    <p>
      The source is therefore not simply an axis value. It is the evidence of a
      design decision at that value.
    </p>

    <aside class="note">
      <p class="note-label">In brief</p>
      <p>
        A design axis names a transformation. A parametric source shows how
        that transformation is drawn.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Expected behaviour</h2>

    <p>
      Each parametric source begins with an expectation: what should change,
      and what should remain stable? This expectation provides a basis for
      designing and evaluating the source.
    </p>

    <p>
      For an <code>XOPQ</code> transformation, for example, the primary change
      concerns the thickness of horizontal stroke components. In many glyphs,
      other aspects of the design can remain stable. In others, related
      adjustments may be needed to preserve proportion, continuity, or
      readability.
    </p>

    <p>
      <em>The documentation records both the intended rule and the cases in which
      the rule needs qualification.</em>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Parameters are related, not isolated</h2>

    <p>
      Parametric design does not require every parameter to act in complete
      isolation. Type design contains connected decisions, and an adjustment to
      one part of a glyph may require a related adjustment elsewhere.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, in a single-stem glyph, an <code>XOPQ</code> adjustment may require a
      corresponding <a href="/reference/parameters/xtra/">XTRA</a> adjustment
      to maintain the intended overall proportion. In a round glyph, changes to stroke
      or proportion may interact with controls for external curvature.
    </p>

    <p>
      These relationships are not failures of the system. They are part of the
      design logic that parametric sources make visible.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>A coordinated source system</h2>

    <p>
      A typeface is not made from isolated sources. Its parametric sources form
      a coordinated system across glyph groups, scripts, styles, and related
      transformations.
    </p>

    <p>
      This system makes it possible to inspect whether a design decision behaves
      consistently across the character set, and to identify the places where
      different forms need their own treatment.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where to go next</h2>

    <p>
      Continue to <a href="/explanation/canonical-axes/">Canonical axes and
      design axes</a> for the framework used to organise transformations. Then
      explore a documented project to see how parametric sources are realised
      in specific glyphs.
    </p>
  </section>
</div>